# XSH getgrnam() and getgrnam_r(), search the group database for a name
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The entry searched for is the one of the caller (real user/group ID) or of ID 0, whichever the database has;
# no particular entry is assumed. Everything is judged on a chain: an entry found by ID must be found again by
# its name, and the reentrant form must agree with the plain form. A lookup that reports one of the listed
# "may fail" errors gives UNRESOLVED. Names are only tried that cannot be a real entry ("pcnoent<pid>x").

assertion  XSH.getgrnam.001
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       getgrnam() with the name of an existing entry returns an entry with
           that name and the same group ID as the entry it was taken from.
test       t/001-chain.c

assertion  XSH.getgrnam.002
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       When no entry has the name, getgrnam() returns a null pointer and does
           not change errno.
note       A change of errno to one of the listed "may fail" errors means the
           lookup failed, not that nothing was found, and gives UNRESOLVED.
test       t/002-absent.c

assertion  XSH.getgrnam.003
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       getgrnam_r() returns 0, stores the address of the structure passed at
           *result, fills it with the entry that getgrnam() returns, and every
           pointer in it refers to storage in the buffer.
test       t/003-r-agrees.c

assertion  XSH.getgrnam.004
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       getgrnam_r() with a name that no entry has returns 0 (not an error)
           and stores a null pointer at *result.
test       t/004-r-absent.c

assertion  XSH.getgrnam.005
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#ERRORS/ERANGE
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       getgrnam_r() with a buffer too small for the entry (1 byte, and 0
           bytes) returns the error number ERANGE and stores a null pointer at
           *result.
note       Any entry needs more than one byte for its name and terminator.
test       t/005-r-erange.c

assertion  XSH.getgrnam.006
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#ERRORS/ERANGE
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       Retrying getgrnam_r() with a buffer twice as large after each ERANGE
           ends with return 0 and the entry, so ERANGE does not repeat without
           bound.
note       The example of the page does this; the buffer is stopped at 1 MiB.
test       t/006-r-retry.c

assertion  XSH.getgrnam.007
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       A name that differs from an existing entry name by one added or
           removed character finds no entry other than one with exactly that name
           (getgrnam() and getgrnam_r()).
note       A negative check: it passes when nothing is found.
test       t/007-exact.c

assertion  XSH.getgrnam.008
anchor     functions/getgrnam.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       sysconf(_SC_GETGR_R_SIZE_MAX) returns either -1 with errno unchanged,
           or a non-negative value.
test       t/008-sysconf.c
